course: world
lesson: 122
title: Clocks before clocks: sundials, water clocks, candles and hourglasses
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Anything that changes at a steady speed can be a clock: a shadow sliding round, water dripping out, a candle burning down, sand trickling through a glass. People used all of them long before springs and batteries.
explain: About 3,500 years ago the ancient Egyptians used shadow clocks and split the day into 12 hours, one reason our clocks still show 12. But a shadow needs sunshine, so for nights and cloudy days people made water clocks: a pot with a small hole and lines inside, the water level falling past a mark each hour. The Greeks called it a clepsydra, "water thief", and gave every speaker in court the same amount of water, just like a debate-club timer.
explain: Candle clocks had hour marks down the side; the story says King Alfred of England used candles that each burned 4 hours, kept in a lantern so draughts couldn't make them burn faster. In China, incense sticks burned slowly, and little balls on threads dropped onto a plate with a clang: an alarm with no electricity.
explain: An hourglass is a timer you can watch: sand falls through a narrow neck. Sailors turned a half-hour glass and rang the ship's bell each time, and eight bells ended a 4-hour watch (a sailor's turn on duty). In 1088 in China, Su Song built a water-powered clock tower over 10 metres high with a turning globe of the stars on top.
explain: Europe's first mechanical clocks, about 700 years ago, often had one hand or none at all. The word "clock" comes from an old word for bell, because they rang the hours: Salisbury Cathedral's clock, from about 1386, still has no face and just strikes the hours.
fact: The ancient Egyptians used shadow clocks about 3,500 years ago, around 1500 BC.
fact: The Egyptians split the day into 12 hours, one reason clocks still show 12 hours.
fact: A water clock lets water drip out at a steady speed, and the level falls past a mark for each hour; it works at night and on cloudy days.
fact: The Greeks called the water clock a clepsydra, meaning "water thief", and used it to give each speaker in court the same time.
fact: Candle clocks had hour marks down the side; King Alfred's candles are said to have burned 4 hours each, so 6 candles lasted a whole day.
fact: In China and Japan, incense clocks burned slowly; balls tied on threads dropped onto a plate at set times, like an alarm.
fact: An hourglass times with sand falling through a narrow neck; hourglasses became common in Europe about 700 years ago.
fact: Sailors turned a half-hour glass and rang the ship's bell each time: 8 bells = 8 × 30 minutes = 4 hours, one watch.
fact: In 1088 in China, Su Song built a water-powered clock tower over 10 metres high that also showed the movement of the stars; that was 938 years before 2026.
fact: Early mechanical clocks in Europe, about 700 years ago, often had only an hour hand.
fact: The word "clock" comes from an old word for bell, "clocca".
fact: The clock at Salisbury Cathedral, built around 1386, is one of the oldest working clocks in the world; it has no face and just strikes the hours.
exercise: About how long ago did the ancient Egyptians use shadow clocks? | About 3,500 years ago | About 350 years ago | About 35,000 years ago | About 100 years ago
exercise: Why do our clocks still show 12 hours? | The Egyptians split the day into 12 hours | There are 12 months in a year, so 12 hours too | The Moon goes round the Earth 12 times a day | A clock face can only fit 12 numbers round it
exercise: How does a water clock tell the time? | Water drips out steadily and its level falls past hour marks | Water is heated until it boils once every hour | Fish swim up to a new mark every hour | Water freezes a little more each hour
exercise: What could a water clock do that a sundial couldn't? | Work at night and on cloudy days | Work only on days when the Sun shines | Ring a loud bell every single minute | Show the date as well as the year
exercise: What does "clepsydra" mean? | Water thief | Sun catcher | Sand keeper | Bell ringer
exercise: Why did courts in ancient Athens, in Greece, use water clocks? | So every speaker got the same amount of time | So the judges knew exactly when their lunch was ready | To keep the court cool in summer | To tell the date of each trial
exercise: Five speakers each get a 6-minute water clock. How long do they speak altogether? | 30 minutes | 11 minutes | 60 minutes | 36 minutes
exercise: King Alfred's candles burned 4 hours each. How many burn through a whole day? | 6 | 4 | 12 | 24
exercise: Why were Alfred's candles kept in a lantern? | So wind couldn't make them burn faster | So they gave out more light for reading | So the melted wax could be saved and used again | So nobody could steal them in the night
exercise: What made an incense clock work like an alarm? | Balls on threads dropped onto a plate when the fire reached them | The smoke turned a different colour every hour | The stick whistled loudly when it burned out | A bird sang when it smelled the smoke
exercise: A ball is tied at the 2-hour mark of an incense clock. When does it drop? | After 2 hours | After 2 minutes | After 20 hours | Straight away
exercise: How many seconds are in 3 minutes, the time of Theo's egg timer? | 180 | 300 | 30 | 120
exercise: On old ships, what did "8 bells" mean? | The end of a 4-hour watch | The end of an 8-hour watch | Eight o'clock in the morning | Danger, everyone up on deck
exercise: Who built a huge water-powered clock tower in China in 1088? | Su Song | Galileo | King Alfred | John Harrison
exercise: How did people read a one-handed church clock? | They looked where the hour hand was between the numbers | They counted the ticks in their heads | They waited for the minute bell to ring | They checked where the shadow of the church tower fell on the ground
exercise: Where does the word "clock" come from? | An old word for bell | An old word for wheel | An old word for sun | An old word for hand
exercise: What is special about the clock at Salisbury Cathedral? | It's one of the oldest working clocks and has no face | It was the first clock with a minute hand | It runs on sand instead of weights | It shows the time in every country
test: Jake: "Shadow clocks 3,500 years ago? That's older than my grandad, lol." How many 70-year lives fit into 3,500 years? | 50 | 5 | 500 | 35
test: Ava, in a museum: "So it's a leaky bucket that tells the time? Bussin'." ("Bussin'" means really good.) What makes it work? | The water drips out at a steady speed past the marks | The water gets heavier every hour | The hole grows bigger every hour | Someone pours in another cupful of water every single hour
test: Mia: "A Greek court with a water clock, that's like debate club, innit." What's the same? | Each speaker gets a fair, equal amount of time | Each speaker has to drink the water | The loudest speaker gets the most time | Speakers may only talk while it is raining outside the court
test: What would happen if a candle clock stood by an open window on a windy night? | It would burn faster and show more time passed than really had | It would burn much slower and show less time passed than really had | It would show exactly the right time | It would stop all the clocks in the house
test: Mei's gran in Singapore: "Incense clock is our old alarm, lah." ("Lah" just adds feeling at the end of a sentence.) How did it make a sound? | Balls on threads fell onto a metal plate | The incense popped loudly every hour | A bell was tied to the smoke | The stick sang as it burned
test: Theo's mum: "Hard-boiled, so two turns of the egg timer, yeah?" Each turn is 3 minutes. How long do the eggs boil? | 6 minutes | 3 minutes | 9 minutes | 2 minutes
test: A pirate cartoon shows the captain ringing "8 bells" because it's 8 o'clock. What did 8 bells really mean? | 8 half-hours had passed: the end of a 4-hour watch | It was exactly eight o'clock in the morning on the ship | Eight enemy ships had just been spotted on the horizon | Eight sailors were needed up on deck to help straight away
test: Jack: "Su Song's clock tower, 1088? That's sick." ("Sick" means brilliant.) How long before 2026 was that? | 938 years | 1,088 years | 838 years | 1,938 years
test: Ruby, from Liverpool: "A clock with one hand? How'd they know the minutes, la?" ("La" means lad or mate.) What's true? | They didn't need exact minutes; "about half past" was fine | They kept a second clock just for minutes | The single hand showed only the minutes | Nobody had heard of hours back then
test: Freya at Salisbury: "No face, no hands? Proper useless, that." Why was it still useful? | It struck a bell for each hour, which anyone could count | It showed the time by the colour of the bell | It was only ever used by the few people who could read and write | It was never meant to tell the time
test: Your mate reckons hourglasses were invented after wristwatches. Put it right. | Hourglasses were common in Europe about 700 years ago, long before wristwatches | He's right, hourglasses came after wristwatches | Hourglasses were first made in the year 2000 | Hourglasses and wristwatches were invented on the same day
exam: Identify the principal advantage of the water clock over the sundial. | Its operation does not depend upon sunshine | Its operation requires no water or other liquid whatsoever | It indicates the date as well as the hour | It strikes a bell at every minute
exam: Given that a ship's half-hour glass is turned eight times in succession, determine the aggregate period measured. | Four hours | Eight hours | Two hours | Forty minutes
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the word "clock". | It derives from an old word signifying "bell" | It derives from an old word signifying "water thief" | It derives from the name of an ancient Egyptian king | It derives from an old word signifying "shadow"
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the clock constructed in 1088 by Su Song? | A water-powered tower exceeding ten metres, displaying the motion of the stars | A pendulum clock of small dimensions for domestic use | A candle clock enclosed within a lantern | A sundial carved upon the wall of a palace
